#!/usr/bin/env sh
# tadw - run this repository's own checks before a push leaves the machine
#
# AGENTS.md lists the checks that run against this repository, and running them
# was a convention rather than a mechanism. The only hard gate here was
# reference-transaction, which fires on `v*` tags alone, so every commit and push
# rode on the session remembering. The stakeholder is the author who pushes a
# broken formatter or a failing suite and finds out late, or never.
#
# A LOCAL HOOK, NOT A HOSTED ONE. Author direction, recorded as D4 in
# docs/plans/quality-gates-hardening.md: .github/workflows/lint.yml keeps the
# checks it runs today and gains nothing new. The gate belongs where the work is,
# so it fails in seconds instead of after a round trip.
#
# THREE BEHAVIORS CARRY THE DESIGN.
#
#   1. Every check runs even after one fails, and all failures report together. A
#      hook that stops at the first failure makes the author push, fail, fix,
#      push, and fail again on the next one.
#   2. A MISSING TOOL WARNS AND ALLOWS, by name. This follows the precedent
#      reference-transaction set for a missing `claude`: an unpushable clone is a
#      worse failure than an unchecked push, and neither rumdl nor node is
#      universally installed. Named, never silent, because a quiet skip would
#      recreate the gap this hook exists to close.
#   3. TADW_PREPUSH=off is a documented escape, the way TADW_STYLE_CORE=off is.
#      An undocumented workaround is one people invent under deadline anyway.
#
# FOUR CHECKS FROM THE AGENTS.md BLOCK ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
#
#   NOTHING UNDER evals/ IS TIED TO A GIT HOOK. Author direction, 2026-08-23, and
#   it covers both eval commands. `python3 evals/run.py` is every case a real model
#   call: far too slow and too expensive to sit on a push. `python3
#   evals/test_run.py` calls no model and costs about 2 seconds, so cost is not why
#   it went: the evals are a measurement you run deliberately, and the hook is not
#   where they belong. They stay in the AGENTS.md list, so the ship gate still runs
#   the harness suite.
#
#   `python3 .githooks/test_prepush.py` is this hook's own suite, and it pushes
#   inside a throwaway fixture whose core.hooksPath points back here. Running it
#   from here would recurse until something gave out.
#
# `claude plugin validate .` is absent for a reason of its own: reference-transaction
# already gates it at the tag, which is the boundary where a broken manifest stops
# being a local mistake, and spawning the claude CLI is the slowest check of the
# set.
#
# This checks the WORKING TREE, not the commits being pushed. That matches how the
# AGENTS.md block is meant to be run by hand, and it is the state the author can
# actually fix right now.
#
# TWO STAGES, ONE FILE. Git calls exactly one pre-push hook, so the two things that
# must happen on this event share this file. Stage 1 runs the checks above. Stage 2
# reads the verdict /quality-gates recorded, and is documented at its own banner
# below. Both stages always run, and each reports under its own message, for the
# same reason stage 1 does not stop at its first failure.

[ "$TADW_PREPUSH" = "off" ] && exit 0

# stdin is one line per ref: <local ref> <local sha> <remote ref> <remote sha>.
# Consume it whether or not we act on it, so git never writes into a closed pipe.
# An all-zero local sha is a deletion, and deleting a remote ref pushes no code to
# check, so a delete-only push has nothing to gate.
#
# The commits themselves are kept, rather than only the fact that there are some:
# stage 2 asks whether the recorded verdict describes what is being pushed, and
# that question needs the shas.
pushing=''
while read -r local_ref local_sha remote_ref remote_sha; do
  case "$local_sha" in
    *[!0]*) pushing="$pushing$local_sha " ;;
  esac
done
[ -n "$pushing" ] || exit 0

# git exports GIT_DIR into every hook it runs, and the value is not portable. From
# the main checkout it is the relative `.git`, which re-resolves under any
# directory a check moves into, so a fixture that runs `git init` in a temp
# directory stays inside that temp directory. From a LINKED WORKTREE it is an
# absolute path to the main repository's gitdir, and `git -C <tmpdir>` does not
# redirect it: the fixture's `git init` re-initializes THIS repository instead.
# Four of the checks below build git fixtures, and one of them inits with
# `-c core.excludesFile=/dev/null`.
#
# That is not hypothetical. A push from a linked worktree left the main checkout
# with core.bare=true and core.excludesFile=/dev/null, which broke every work-tree
# git operation in both checkouts until it was repaired by hand.
#
# Clear the inherited state before the first git call, so git rediscovers the
# repository from the hook's own working directory, which git sets to the worktree
# root. GIT_PREFIX is included because it is a path fragment relative to the gitdir
# it came with, and it means nothing once that gitdir is gone.
unset GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE GIT_INDEX_FILE GIT_COMMON_DIR GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY GIT_PREFIX

root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
cd "$root" || exit 0

work=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tadw-prepush.$$
mkdir -p "$work" || exit 0

total=0
ran=0
failed=0
skipped=''
job_plan=''
job_pids=''
after_previous=''
previous_line_planned=''

# THE CHECKS RUN AT THE SAME TIME, so a push waits for the slowest check rather
# than for the sum. `check` only plans: it records the command and assigns it
# to a job. The jobs start after the last `check` line. The report reads each
# check's numbered files in list order, so it reads the same as a serial run's.
#
# `after_previous=yes` on the line above a `check` line puts that check in the
# job of the check on the line above, so the two run one after the other. When
# the check above was skipped for a missing tool, there is nothing to take turns
# with, and this check gets a job of its own.
check() {
  total=$((total + 1))
  if command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    plan_check "$@"
    previous_line_planned=yes
  else
    note_skipped_tool "$1"
    previous_line_planned=''
  fi
  after_previous=''
  return 0
}

# Deduplicated, because python3 carries most of these checks and one warning
# about it is the useful number. Not a count on purpose: the earlier wording
# said "six" and was stale at eight.
note_skipped_tool() {
  case " $skipped " in
    *" $1 "*) ;;
    *) skipped="$skipped$1 " ;;
  esac
}

# Creates the check's files here, in the hook's own shell, where a failure to
# write one prints a visible error. The job silences its own stderr later.
# A job is its check numbers joined by ':'; the plan is the jobs, space-separated.
plan_check() {
  ran=$((ran + 1))
  printf '%s\n' "$*" >"$work/$total.cmd"
  : >"$work/$total.out"
  : >"$work/$total.rc"
  if [ "$after_previous" = yes ] && [ -n "$previous_line_planned" ]; then
    job_plan="$job_plan:$total"
  else
    job_plan="$job_plan $total"
  fi
}

# One job per processor at most. Past that the checks compete for the CPU, and
# the ones that wait on a timeout or a race fail a clean push.
job_limit() {
  limit=$(getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null)
  case $limit in
    '' | *[!0-9]* | 0) echo 4 ;;
    *) echo "$limit" ;;
  esac
}

start_jobs() {
  limit=$(job_limit)
  for job in $job_plan; do
    # shellcheck disable=SC2046,SC2086 # one word per pid, split on purpose
    while [ "$(count_words $(running_among $job_pids))" -ge "$limit" ]; do
      pause
    done
    run_job "$job" &
    job_pids="$job_pids $!"
  done
}

# The body of one background job: its checks, one after the other. A check
# ended by a signal ends the job too, so an interrupt starts no later check.
run_job() {
  # Every file this job writes already exists, so this stream carries only the
  # shell's notices, such as "Terminated" when an interrupt stops a check.
  exec 2>/dev/null
  IFS=:
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086 # split on ':' on purpose
  set -- $1
  unset IFS
  for number in "$@"; do
    # A check's arguments are single words. test_prepush.py splits them the
    # same way; -f keeps a word from expanding as a glob.
    set -f
    # shellcheck disable=SC2046
    set -- $(cat "$work/$number.cmd")
    set +f
    with_default_signals "$@" >"$work/$number.out" 2>&1
    code=$?
    echo "$code" >"$work/$number.rc"
    [ "$code" -gt 128 ] && exit "$code"
  done
}

# A non-interactive shell starts every background job with SIGINT ignored, and
# a program inherits that through exec. So a Python check would neither stop on
# Ctrl-C nor run its own cleanup, and sh has no way to undo it. python3 resets
# the signal and then becomes the check. It also resets SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ,
# which Python itself ignores and would pass on the same way. Without python3,
# the check runs as it is, and the TERM after the grace period stops it.
with_default_signals() {
  if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    python3 -c '
import os, signal, sys
for name in ("SIGINT", "SIGPIPE", "SIGXFSZ"):
    if hasattr(signal, name):
        signal.signal(getattr(signal, name), signal.SIG_DFL)
os.execvp(sys.argv[1], sys.argv[1:])
' "$@"
  else
    "$@"
  fi
}

# Prints each pid that is still running. A finished job can stay a zombie
# until its shell reaps it, and `kill -0` succeeds on a zombie, so this reads
# the process state instead. `ps -p` takes a comma-separated list.
running_among() {
  [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  pid_list=$1
  shift
  for pid in "$@"; do
    pid_list="$pid_list,$pid"
  done
  ps -o pid=,stat= -p "$pid_list" 2>/dev/null | while read -r pid state; do
    case $state in
      Z*) ;;
      *) echo "$pid" ;;
    esac
  done
}

count_words() {
  echo "$#"
}

# A fraction of a second is not POSIX, but macOS, GNU, and busybox `sleep` all
# take one, and a whole second would add to every push.
pause() {
  sleep 0.1 2>/dev/null || sleep 1
}

# FREEZE, THEN ASK, THEN INSIST.
#
# Freeze: SIGSTOP every process under this hook, then look again, until a look
# finds nothing new. A stopped process cannot start another, so this also finds
# the processes started while the hook was looking.
#
# Ask: SIGINT to every frozen check, and SIGTERM to the job shells, which ignore
# SIGINT and would otherwise start the next check in their job. A Python check
# then runs its cleanup: its `finally` blocks, its atexit handlers, and the
# servers it started in sessions of their own.
#
# Insist: whatever still runs after a grace period is frozen again, together
# with every process it started during the grace period, and gets SIGTERM.
# That second search starts from the survivors, not from this hook: their job
# shells are gone by then, so the survivors are no longer under this hook.
#
# The first search walks this hook's own process tree rather than a list of job
# pids, so a job that started a moment before the signal is found too.
stop_checks() {
  frozen=''
  freeze_under "$$"
  [ -n "$frozen" ] || return 0

  job_shells=$(children_of "$$")
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086 # one word per pid, split on purpose
  kill -INT $frozen 2>/dev/null
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -TERM $job_shells 2>/dev/null
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -CONT $frozen 2>/dev/null

  tries=0
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  while [ "$tries" -lt 50 ] && [ -n "$(running_among $frozen)" ]; do
    pause
    tries=$((tries + 1))
  done

  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  survivors=$(running_among $frozen)
  [ -n "$survivors" ] || return 0
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -STOP $survivors 2>/dev/null
  # One space between pids, because freeze_under matches a pid by the spaces
  # around it, and running_among prints one pid per line.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  set -- $survivors
  frozen=" $*"
  freeze_under "$@"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -TERM $frozen 2>/dev/null
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  kill -CONT $frozen 2>/dev/null
}

# Sends SIGSTOP to every process under the given pids and adds each to $frozen,
# looking again until a look finds nothing new.
freeze_under() {
  while :; do
    unseen=''
    for pid in $(descendants_of "$@"); do
      # The walk's own subshells are children of this hook too, but they have
      # already exited here, and without this test each walk finds new ones.
      kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null || continue
      case " $frozen " in
        *" $pid "*) ;;
        *) unseen="$unseen $pid" ;;
      esac
    done
    [ -n "$unseen" ] || return 0
    # shellcheck disable=SC2086
    kill -STOP $unseen 2>/dev/null
    frozen="$frozen $unseen"
  done
}

# Every process as a pid and its parent's pid, one pair per line. One reading
# per search: a `pgrep` for each process made one search take seconds once a
# check had started a few hundred children.
process_table() {
  ps -A -o pid= -o ppid= 2>/dev/null
}

children_of() {
  process_table | while read -r pid parent; do
    if [ "$parent" = "$1" ]; then echo "$pid"; fi
  done
}

# Prints every pid under the given pids, at any depth.
descendants_of() {
  table=$(process_table)
  under=" $* "
  grew=yes
  while [ -n "$grew" ]; do
    grew=''
    while read -r pid parent; do
      case $under in
        *" $pid "*) ;;
        *" $parent "*) under="$under$pid " grew=yes ;;
      esac
    done <<EOF
$table
EOF
  done
  for pid in $under; do
    case " $* " in
      *" $pid "*) ;;
      *) echo "$pid" ;;
    esac
  done
}

# Exits on a signal rather than returning to the script. A parallel hook that
# fell through would read no status files and report that the checks passed.
# stop_checks writes to /dev/null, so the shell's own notices about the jobs
# it stops never reach the author.
trap 'rm -rf "$work"' EXIT
trap 'stop_checks 2>/dev/null; exit 129' HUP
trap 'stop_checks 2>/dev/null; exit 130' INT
trap 'stop_checks 2>/dev/null; exit 143' TERM

started=$(date +%s)

# In the AGENTS.md order, so the two lists read as one list.
# test_prepush.py asserts they stay that way.
check rumdl fmt --check .
check rumdl check . --extend-disable MD013
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/check_markdown_wrap.py
check node hooks/test-hooks.js
check bash hooks/test-claude-scripts.sh
check python3 skills/style-testing/scripts/test_check_framework_leak.py
check python3 skills/style-testing/scripts/check_framework_leak.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_check_doc_paths.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/check_doc_paths.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_changed_set.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_check_hygiene.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_route_qa.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_probe_api.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_write_report_json.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_check_documented_bd_commands.py
# Both of these run the real bd, which opens the embedded Dolt database under
# .beads/ inside each process, so they take turns rather than contend for it.
after_previous=yes
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/check_documented_bd_commands.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/test_check_documented_paths.py
check python3 skills/quality-gates/scripts/check_documented_paths.py
check python3 skills/product-surface-docs/scripts/test_check_drive_blocks.py
check python3 skills/product-surface-docs/scripts/check_drive_blocks.py
check python3 skills/bead-refine/scripts/test_check_round_format.py
check python3 skills/bead-refine/scripts/check_round_format.py
check python3 skills/ship/scripts/test_check_worktree_occupants.py
check python3 skills/ship/scripts/test_resolve_ground.py
check python3 skills/ship/scripts/test_landed_check.py
check python3 skills/ship/scripts/test_read_gate_config.py
check python3 skills/ship/scripts/test_measure_gate_baseline.py
check python3 skills/ship/scripts/test_resolve_rebase_conflict.py
check python3 skills/reconcile-acceptance/scripts/test_load_findings.py
check python3 skills/reconcile-quality-gates/scripts/test_load_findings.py
check python3 skills/verify-acceptance/scripts/test_write_acceptance_report.py

start_jobs
wait

# The checks are done, and stages 2 and 3 start no background job, so from here
# a signal only ends the hook, which leaves stage 3 no chance to commit. A trap
# that returned to the script would let it go on to commit after the interrupt.
trap 'exit 129' HUP
trap 'exit 130' INT
trap 'exit 143' TERM

number=0
while [ "$number" -lt "$total" ]; do
  number=$((number + 1))
  # No command file means the check never started, because its tool is missing.
  [ -e "$work/$number.cmd" ] || continue
  status=$(cat "$work/$number.rc" 2>/dev/null)
  [ "$status" = 0 ] && continue
  failed=$((failed + 1))
  {
    printf '\n----- FAILED: %s\n' "$(cat "$work/$number.cmd")"
    cat "$work/$number.out" 2>/dev/null
    # An empty status file means the check never finished, so it verified
    # nothing: its job was killed, or it could not write its own files.
    [ -n "$status" ] || echo "the check recorded no exit status, so it did not finish"
  } >>"$work/report"
done

elapsed=$(( $(date +%s) - started ))

if [ -n "$skipped" ]; then
  echo "tadw: WARNING - not on PATH, so its checks were skipped: $skipped" >&2
fi

# Recorded rather than exited on, because stage 2 has its own question to answer
# and a reader deserves both answers from one push. Read at the very bottom.
suite_failed=0

if [ "$ran" -eq 0 ]; then
  # Allowed, because every tool being absent is still a missing tool, and the rule
  # above is deliberate. Never reported as a pass, though: nothing was verified,
  # and "checks passed" is the one sentence a reader must not see here. This is
  # the all-skipped-run-reports-PASS shape the quality-gates skill refuses by name.
  echo "tadw: WARNING - 0 of $total checks ran, so nothing was verified" >&2
elif [ "$failed" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "tadw: pre-push checks passed ($ran of $total, ${elapsed}s)" >&2
else
  cat "$work/report" >&2
  echo "" >&2
  echo "tadw: refusing to push, $failed of $ran checks failed (${elapsed}s)" >&2
  echo "tadw: fix the failures above, or set TADW_PREPUSH=off to skip this hook" >&2
  suite_failed=1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STAGE 2: the verdict /quality-gates recorded
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The gates produce a markdown report and, since the artifact bead, a JSON copy of
# their verdict. Nothing read it. "Landing the Plane" in AGENTS.md tells a session
# to run the gates before pushing, and a session that ran them, got a FAIL, and
# pushed anyway hit no barrier and left no trace.
#
# THE PATH IS RESOLVED, NEVER HARDCODED. `git rev-parse --git-dir` returns a
# distinct path per linked worktree, so each worktree's hook reads that worktree's
# own verdict about that worktree's own branch. A literal `.git/` is a file rather
# than a directory inside a worktree, and `--git-common-dir` would let whichever
# worktree ran the gates last decide every worktree's push. The skill resolves the
# write the same way and for the same reason.
#
# FORGIVENESS IS THE DESIGN, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
#
#   No report, or one that cannot be read, warns and allows. Absence is not
#   evidence of a problem, and blocking there would refuse every documentation
#   push from a fresh clone and teach people to turn the hook off.
#
#   Only a recorded verdict of FAIL blocks, and the message carries the verdict,
#   the head it was recorded for, and when. Both exits are named in it, because a
#   gate whose way out you have to guess gets bypassed with --no-verify instead.
#
#   A verdict recorded for a commit that is not in what you are pushing warns as
#   stale and allows. It describes some other tree, so it is evidence about
#   nothing here, and a FAIL still blocks in that state on purpose: refreshing it
#   is one command, and a stale FAIL is exactly when you want to be asked.

report=$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/quality-gates-report.json

# In python3 because sh cannot parse JSON, and hand-rolled matching would read a
# verdict out of a string that merely contains one. Four lines out: a status, then
# the three facts the FAIL message has to name. Each field is one whole line, so a
# value carrying a space cannot shift the field after it.
python3 - "$report" >"$work/verdict" 2>/dev/null <<'PY'
import json
import sys


def one_line(value):
    text = "" if value is None else str(value).strip()
    return text.splitlines()[0] if text else "-"


def emit(status, verdict=None, head=None, timestamp=None):
    print(status)
    print(one_line(verdict).upper())
    print(one_line(head))
    print(one_line(timestamp))
    raise SystemExit(0)


try:
    with open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        recorded = json.load(handle)
except FileNotFoundError:
    emit("missing")
except (OSError, ValueError):
    emit("unreadable")

if not isinstance(recorded, dict):
    emit("unreadable")
emit("ok", recorded.get("verdict"), recorded.get("head"), recorded.get("timestamp"))
PY

status=''
verdict=''
head=''
stamp=''
{ read -r status; read -r verdict; read -r head; read -r stamp; } <"$work/verdict"
# Empty means the reader never ran, which is python3 missing from PATH. Stage 1
# already named it as a skipped tool; this says what its absence cost here.
[ -n "$status" ] || status='unreadable'

verdict_failed=0

case $status in
  ok) ;;
  missing)
    echo "tadw: WARNING - no quality-gates verdict recorded at $report; run /quality-gates to record one" >&2
    ;;
  *)
    echo "tadw: WARNING - the quality-gates verdict at $report could not be read, so this push is not gated on it" >&2
    ;;
esac

if [ "$status" = 'ok' ] && [ "$verdict" = 'FAIL' ]; then
  echo "tadw: the recorded quality-gates verdict is FAIL" >&2
  echo "tadw:   recorded head: $head" >&2
  echo "tadw:   recorded at:   $stamp" >&2
  echo "tadw: refusing to push on that verdict. Re-run /quality-gates to record a" >&2
  echo "tadw: fresh one, or set TADW_PREPUSH=off to skip this hook" >&2
  verdict_failed=1
elif [ "$status" = 'ok' ]; then
  # Stale unless the recorded head is an ancestor of something being pushed. An
  # unknown object fails --is-ancestor, which is the right answer: a verdict about
  # a commit this clone has never seen describes some other tree too.
  stale='yes'
  for sha in $pushing; do
    if git merge-base --is-ancestor "$head" "$sha" 2>/dev/null; then
      stale=''
      break
    fi
  done
  if [ -n "$stale" ]; then
    echo "tadw: WARNING - the recorded quality-gates verdict ($verdict, $stamp) is stale: it was recorded for $head, which is not in what you are pushing" >&2
  fi
fi

if [ "$suite_failed" -eq 1 ] || [ "$verdict_failed" -eq 1 ]; then
  exit 1
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STAGE 3: keep .beads/issues.jsonl current, silently
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# AGENTS.md's "The author never handles tracker plumbing" says a person never
# runs `bd export` by hand, and an agent that needs it current runs it itself.
# That depended on every agent remembering. This stage makes it automatic: a
# push now exports the tracker and commits the change, so a person who edits a
# bead with no agent in the loop still pushes current state (tadw-pm8).
#
# NEVER A GATE. `bd` missing, or `bd export` failing, only warns; a stale
# export is a worse outcome than a blocked push. Only stage 1 and stage 2 can
# refuse a push.
#
# A FOLLOW-UP COMMIT, NEVER AN AMEND. Git resolves which commit to push before
# invoking this hook and does not re-read the ref afterward, so a commit made
# here cannot join THIS push; it advances local HEAD by one commit that goes
# out on the NEXT push. tadw-pm8's own design records amending as the
# alternative, and rejects it: it would push a stale SHA unless the hook
# re-pushed itself, which is not obviously safe. `/tadw:ship`'s own Step 4
# already names a hook-added commit as the accepted outcome for this case:
# "Leave it, and name it in the report."
#
# THE WORKING TREE, NOT THE COMMITS BEING PUSHED, matching stage 1's own rule
# above. Whichever branch is checked out here is the one that gets the commit,
# regardless of which ref the push argument names.
#
# THE TEXT EXPORT ONLY, NOT THE DOLT REMOTE. tadw-pm8 measured `bd dolt remote
# list` and `bd dolt show` disagreeing about whether one exists, so no bead
# data has ever synced that way. That sync stays a manual `bd dolt push` until
# the disagreement is resolved; this stage never attempts it.
if ! command -v bd >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo "tadw: WARNING - bd not on PATH, so the tracker export was skipped and this push does not carry current tracker state" >&2
elif ! bd export -o .beads/issues.jsonl >"$work/bd-export.out" 2>&1; then
  echo "tadw: WARNING - bd export failed, so this push does not carry current tracker state and beads now exist on one machine only" >&2
  cat "$work/bd-export.out" >&2
elif [ -n "$(git status --porcelain .beads/ 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
  git add .beads/ >/dev/null 2>&1
  if ! git commit -q -m "chore(beads): refresh tracker export" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo "tadw: WARNING - bd export changed .beads/ but committing it failed, so this push does not carry current tracker state" >&2
  fi
fi

exit 0
